--TEST--
`Core\Csv::parse` and `::format` accept and refuse the same dialects, asserted by agreement over a table rather than by either member's own answer, and the one asymmetry is the `escape` byte only the reader has — spec § 12's second table
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `csv-honours-a-non-default-dialect-and-refuses-a-bad-one.nvst` asks each
// member about a dialect of its own, which a member that grew its own idea of
// what a dialect is would still pass. The claim
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/csv.rs`'s module doc makes is stronger: there is
// **one** dialect rule — a single ASCII byte each, never `CR` or `LF`, and no
// two of them the same — and both members are asked it. So every dialect below
// is put to both, and what is counted is that the two answered the same thing.
final class Ask {
    public static function parses(string $sep, string $quote): bool {
        try {
            Core\Csv::parse("a,b\n", {separator: $sep, quote: $quote});
            return true;
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    public static function formats(string $sep, string $quote): bool {
        try {
            Core\Csv::format([["a", "b"]], {separator: $sep, quote: $quote});
            return true;
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return false;
        }
    }
}

// Three dialects with a reading — the default and two the caller wrote — then
// the six ways of having none: the separator repeated as the quote under two
// different bytes, a line feed and a carriage return where a record already
// ends, two characters where one byte is compared, and nothing at all.
array<string> $seps = [",", ";", "|", ",", ";", "\n", ",", ";;", ""];
array<string> $quotes = ["\"", "'", "\"", ",", ";", "\"", "\r", "\"", "\""];

int $n = 0;
int $agree = 0;
int $accepted = 0;
int $i = 0;
for (; $i < 9; $i = $i + 1) {
    string $sep = $seps[$i as string];
    string $quote = $quotes[$i as string];
    bool $read = Ask::parses($sep, $quote);
    bool $wrote = Ask::formats($sep, $quote);
    $n = $n + 1;
    if ($read == $wrote) {
        $agree = $agree + 1;
    }
    if ($read) {
        $accepted = $accepted + 1;
    }
}

echo "dialects ", $n, "\n";
echo "agree ", $agree, "\n";
echo "accepted ", $accepted, "\n";

// The asymmetry, and it is the only one: `parse` holds a third byte to the
// same rule, because an escape character is something a *reader* acts on.
// `format` has no `escape` option at all — the module doc owns why — so this
// is not a dialect the two disagree about, it is a dialect only one of them
// can be handed. Both halves of the three-way check are named here; the
// two-way half is the sweep above.
try {
    Core\Csv::parse("a,b\n", {escape: ","});
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "escape is the separator: refused\n";
}
try {
    Core\Csv::parse("a,b\n", {escape: "\""});
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    echo "escape is the quote: refused\n";
}
// And a third distinct byte is a dialect, which is what makes the two refusals
// above the rule rather than `escape` being unusable.
echo "escape is its own byte: ", Core\Arr::count(Core\Csv::parse("a,b\n", {escape: "\\"})), " record\n";
--EXPECT--
dialects 9
agree 9
accepted 3
escape is the separator: refused
escape is the quote: refused
escape is its own byte: 1 record
